This paper aims to investigate the exponential growth-exhibiting biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  &\Delta^2u=t\mathcal{Q}u+\lambda u+\beta\left(\frac{1}{\mathbb{R}^N}\int_{\mathbb{R}^N}(\mathcal{K}^2)^s\,dy\,f(\mathbb{R}^N,\,u(\mathbb{R}^N,\,y))\,dy\right)\mathbb{R}^N\,u+\beta\mathbb{R}^N\,|u|^2\,u.
\end{align*} Under specific conditions on the parameters $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $s$, we demonstrate the existence of a unique solution that is within $C^2(\mathbb{R}^N)$ and is a minimum point for the energy functional within the constrained space $C^2_0(\mathbb{R}^N)$. Furthermore, our findings extend standard variational methods by exploring key properties of the biharmonic Choquard type equation, as well as establishing important estimates that facilitate the proof of our results.
